(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-1-general-dermatology-i-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"General Dermatology I Quiz 2 — CMS I Exam 1","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"What is the relationship between the terms dermatitis and eczema?","choices":["Dermatitis is infectious and eczema is inflammatory","Dermatitis is a broad term for inflammatory skin disorders; eczema describes a pruritic inflammatory reaction pattern, and the two are used interchangeably","Eczema is the broad category for inflammatory skin disorders and dermatitis is one pruritic subtype within it, so the two should never be used interchangeably","They describe the same condition at different ages"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Atopic dermatitis is the commonest eczema, which is why 'eczema' often means it. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 46"},{"q":"Nail clipping with periodic acid-Schiff stain evaluates which suspected condition?","choices":["Alopecia areata","Onychomycosis","Psoriatic nail disease","Subungual melanoma"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"What is the usual application schedule for topical corticosteroids given in this lecture?","choices":["Twice a day indefinitely for chronic conditions","Twice a day for two weeks","Once a day for six weeks","Three times a day until the rash resolves"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, with potency selected according to site and severity. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"How do seborrhoeic dermatitis lesions appear, and where?","choices":["Well-demarcated salmon plaques with prominent silvery scale on the extensor elbows, knees, trunk, scalp, umbilicus and sacrum","Coin-shaped plaques without central clearing on the extremities","Tense bullae on the trunk and flexures","Poorly demarcated pruritic erythematous plaques 5 to 20 mm, greasy with flaking or yellow scaling, in areas rich in sebaceous glands"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — scalp, eyebrows, under facial hair, nasolabial creases, forehead, nose, behind the ears and the external auditory canal. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 94"},{"q":"How are severe or extensive allergic contact dermatitis eruptions treated?","choices":["High-dose oral corticosteroids tapered over two to three weeks, because early discontinuation causes high rates of rebound","High-potency topical steroids alone regardless of how extensive the eruption is across the body surface area","A five-day course of oral corticosteroids without taper","Oral antihistamines alone"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Limited eruptions can be managed with high-potency topical steroids. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 89"},{"q":"A mineral oil preparation is used to confirm which diagnosis?","choices":["Bullous pemphigoid, by identifying basement membrane antibodies","Allergic contact dermatitis, by identifying the causative allergen","Scabies infestation, by identifying the mite, its eggs or its faecal pellets","Onychomycosis, by identifying fungal elements in the nail"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — a rapid bedside test. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 33"},{"q":"Who most commonly develops nummular eczema?","choices":["Infants and young children","Adolescents in the weeks following a streptococcal throat infection","Men, and more often adults over the age of 50, though it can occur at any age","Women in their twenties and thirties"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. It is likely caused by some degree of skin barrier dysfunction. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 71"},{"q":"What is first-line treatment for alopecia areata by age group?","choices":["Systemic corticosteroids in all age groups","Intralesional steroids in adolescents and adults; topical steroids in children aged ten and under","Topical minoxidil at every age","Oral finasteride in adults and topical minoxidil in children under the age of ten years"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, and psychological support is named as one of the most important considerations. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 140"},{"q":"Which finding should raise concern for deep venous thrombosis in a patient with suspected stasis dermatitis?","choices":["Atrophie blanche","Acute unilateral swelling or pain","Bilateral chronic hyperpigmentation","Pruritus with scaling"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Other considerations include peripheral arterial disease, vasculitis, lymphoedema and cutaneous malignancy. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 115"},{"q":"What distinguishes an ulceration from an erosion?","choices":["An ulceration is full-thickness loss extending to the dermis or deeper; an erosion loses only the epidermis","An ulceration is linear and an erosion is round","An ulceration is painful and an erosion is painless","An ulceration involves only the epidermis and always heals without scarring, while an erosion reaches the dermis or deeper"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — depth is the discriminator, and it determines whether the lesion can scar. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"At what level does blistering occur in bullous pemphigoid, and who does it affect?","choices":["Intraepithelial, from Malassezia overgrowth, in middle-aged men","Subepithelial, from autoimmune damage to the epithelial basement membrane, twice as often in men, typically after age 60","Intraepithelial, from loss of keratinocyte adhesion, in young adults","Subepithelial, from a delayed type IV hypersensitivity reaction, most often in children and young adults"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — a relatively benign disease usually remitting in five to six years. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 122"},{"q":"What causes irritant contact dermatitis, and how common is it relative to the allergic form?","choices":["An immunoglobulin E-mediated immediate hypersensitivity reaction, and it is the most common form of contact dermatitis","Overgrowth of Malassezia, and it is the most common form","A response to chemicals or friction that disrupts the skin barrier, and it is the most common form of contact dermatitis","A delayed type IV hypersensitivity reaction, and it is the less common form"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — frequently occupational, as in healthcare and janitorial staff who wash their hands often. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 80"},{"q":"How is pityriasis rosea treated?","choices":["It is self-limited, so reassurance is the mainstay, with antihistamines or topical steroids used cautiously for pruritus","A tapering course of oral prednisone given over a period of two to three weeks from the outset of the eruption","Topical ketoconazole","Oral doxycycline for six weeks"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Ultraviolet B phototherapy or sunlight helps if begun in the first week; severe cases may be treated with acyclovir. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 171"},{"q":"What is the Auspitz sign?","choices":["Lesions appear at sites of trauma","The top layers of skin slough when rubbed","Fine white lines on the surface of a plaque","Removal of scale produces bleeding"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — a classic psoriasis finding, alongside Koebner's phenomenon. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 159"},{"q":"How are nummular eczema plaques described?","choices":["Intensely pruritic, round coin-shaped, light pink, scaly, thin plaques 1 to 10 cm, uniform without central clearing","Well-demarcated salmon-pink plaques with prominent silvery scale on the extensor surfaces of the elbows and knees","Greasy yellow scaling plaques in sebum-rich areas","Violaceous flat-topped polygonal papules with fine white lines"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, most commonly on the extremities though they can occur on the trunk. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 72"},{"q":"How do male- and female-pattern hair loss differ in distribution?","choices":["Male pattern recedes at the frontotemporal hairline in a triangular pattern then the vertex; female pattern thins diffusely in central and parietal regions without significant frontal hairline change","Both begin at the vertex and spread forward","Male pattern produces diffuse thinning of the central and parietal scalp while female pattern recedes at the frontotemporal hairline in a triangular shape then the vertex","Both produce discrete round patches"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — preservation of the frontal hairline is the female-pattern hallmark. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 146"},{"q":"What is the mechanism of pemphigus?","choices":["Loss of keratinocyte-to-keratinocyte adhesion, called acantholysis, from autoantibodies to adhesion molecules, producing intraepithelial blisters","Autoimmune damage to the epithelial basement membrane producing subepithelial blistering that resolves without any scarring","A type IV delayed hypersensitivity reaction to an external allergen","Impaired stratum-corneum hydration"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct — a life-threatening blistering disorder of skin and mucous membranes. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 129"},{"q":"What is lichenification?","choices":["A break in the skin","Abnormal formation of connective tissue","Thickening and hardening of the skin","Accumulation of loose cornified fragments of the epidermis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — it develops from chronic rubbing or scratching. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"What proportion of children worldwide are affected by atopic dermatitis, and in which sex is it more common?","choices":["About 20% of children, more common in females","About 5% of children, more common in males","About 40% of children, with no sex difference","About 20% of children, more common in males"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. It often begins in infancy or childhood; adult-onset disease occurs but is rare. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"What is the foundation of treatment for diaper dermatitis?","choices":["Oral antibiotics to prevent secondary infection","A high-potency topical corticosteroid until resolution","Reducing moisture and irritant exposure through frequent changes, gentle cleansing, air exposure and superabsorbent diapers, with a thick zinc oxide or petrolatum barrier at each change","A topical antifungal applied at every diaper change regardless of cause, together with a course of oral antibiotics to prevent secondary bacterial infection of the damaged barrier"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The previous barrier layer need not be scrubbed away if it remains clean. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 109"},{"q":"Which six features are used to translate a dermatological finding into words?","choices":["Primary morphology, secondary morphology, demarcation, colour, size and distribution","Primary morphology, colour and distribution only, since the rest are subjective","Onset, duration, severity, triggers, treatments tried and response","Location, laterality, symmetry, tenderness, temperature and texture"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Getting all six into a description is what lets another provider picture the lesion without seeing it. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"Which finding is pathognomonic for alopecia areata?","choices":["Diffuse thinning of the central scalp","Receding frontotemporal hairline","Exclamation point hairs","Broken hairs close to the scalp with scaling"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct — thinner at the root and normal calibre at the top, because growth is changing in real time. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 138"},{"q":"What is first-line treatment for dyshidrotic eczema?","choices":["High-potency topical corticosteroids","Low-potency topical corticosteroids","Topical antifungals","Oral antibiotics"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, with systemic corticosteroids reserved for severe cases. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"What defines a wheal?","choices":["A fluid-filled lesion containing leukocytes","A fluid-filled lesion under one centimetre","A deposit of blood in the skin measuring one to two millimetres across","A firm oedematous plaque produced by infiltration of fluid into the dermis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct — the fluid is in the tissue rather than in a cavity, which is what separates it from a vesicle. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"What are Wickham's striae?","choices":["Silvery scale on psoriatic plaques","Linear excoriations from scratching","Atrophic stretch marks from prolonged topical steroid use","Fine white lines visible on the surface of lichen planus plaques"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. On the buccal mucosa oral lichen planus appears as lacey white lesions. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 176"},{"q":"When should herpes simplex virus polymerase chain reaction be ordered in atopic dermatitis?","choices":["Only when the patient has known herpes simplex","Only in adult-onset disease","For painful monomorphic erosions or systemic illness","For every flare, since secondary infection is common"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Culture is reserved instead for purulent, pustular or significantly crusted lesions. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"How is pemphigus initially treated?","choices":["With ultrapotent topical steroids alone","With watchful observation alone, since the disease usually remits spontaneously within five to six years without treatment","With oral doxycycline as monotherapy","Urgently, with rituximab or high-dose oral prednisone, often alongside azathioprine or mycophenolate so steroids can be tapered off"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. It is a life-threatening disorder, so treatment is urgent. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 133"},{"q":"Which medications can produce lichenoid reactions?","choices":["Topical corticosteroids","Nonsteroidal anti-inflammatory drugs, sulfonamides, tetracyclines, hydrochlorothiazide, quinidine and some beta blockers","Penicillins, cephalosporins and the macrolide antibiotics, taken either together as a class or individually","Statins and proton pump inhibitors"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — worth asking about, since the eruption can mimic idiopathic lichen planus. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 173"},{"q":"How are petechiae and purpura distinguished by size in this lecture?","choices":["Petechiae are under 3 mm and purpura are 3 mm to 1 cm","Petechiae are 4 mm or greater and purpura are 1 to 2 mm, the reverse of the usual","Both are defined as deposits of blood greater than 1 cm","Petechiae are deposits of blood 1 to 2 mm; purpura are 4 mm or greater"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, and purpura is flagged as a medical emergency until proven otherwise. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"Which biopsy technique cuts deep into the fat layers and is preferred for rashes or deeper bumps?","choices":["Curettage","Punch biopsy","Shave biopsy","Excisional biopsy"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct — it often needs one or two stitches. — 2. General Dermatology I.pptx, Slide 26"}]};
